    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66074,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Giant pigmented naevus NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66075,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Giant congenital melanocytic naevus with garment distribution frequently affects the genitalia. Small lesions can involve the vulva.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66076,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital melanocytic naevi are well-defined (but not sharply demarcated), tan to brown papules, nodules, or plaques. Within the first 6&nbsp;years, congenital melanocytic naevi may continue to grow and change colour {3777972}. They usually darken, get thicker, and increase the volume of terminal hairs. Satellitosis is often seen. Most congenital melanocytic naevi arise in the vulvar skin. Small congenital naevi are &lt;&nbsp;1.5&nbsp;cm in diameter, intermediate congenital naevi 1.5&ndash;20&nbsp;cm, and giant congenital naevi &gt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;cm {23182059; 19503980}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66077,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Small congenital melanocytic naevi affect approximately 1% of newborn infants.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66078,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66079,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most large congenital melanocytic naevi harbour exon&nbsp;3 <em>NRAS</em> mutations; smaller congenital melanocytic naevi can also harbour <em>BRAF</em> p.Val600Glu mutations {31111470}. Congenital naevi are related to the migration of neural crest precursors (melanoblasts) during embryogenesis {24460190}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66080,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66081,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Junctional congenital melanocytic naevi display intraepidermal single and nested melanocytes in a background of variably hyperplastic epidermis. The dermal and compound types exhibit single and nested small dermal melanocytes lacking cytological atypia and demonstrating maturation. Perivascular and periadnexal growth can be noted. Melanocytes can be seen infiltrating arrector pili and/or nerves. If biopsy is performed within the first weeks of life, during the naevi&rsquo;s active growth phase, a concerning pagetoid scatter and high dermal mitotic activity (as many as 27&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>) can be present {23348144}. Longstanding congenital melanocytic naevi can develop dermal proliferating nodules, some of which are composed of atypical melanocytes and show mitotic activity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66082,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 451,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:39:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
